WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.129
The sequence one over n appears to approach zero

00:00:04.129 --> 00:00:14.900
A convergence proof turns that picture into a promise: for every positive error tolerance, eventually every remaining term lies within that distance of the proposed limit.

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:18.668
Choose a tolerance of one tenth

00:00:18.668 --> 00:00:26.896
Starting at n equals eleven, every term is smaller than one tenth because increasing the positive denominator decreases the fraction

00:00:26.896 --> 00:00:31.833
Checking one small term alone would not control the whole remaining tail.

00:00:33.133 --> 00:00:38.357
For an arbitrary positive epsilon, choose an integer N larger than its reciprocal

00:00:38.357 --> 00:00:46.394
Whenever n is at least N, one over n is at most one over N, which is smaller than epsilon

00:00:46.394 --> 00:00:47.600
That proves convergence.

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:51.650
Boundedness alone is weaker than convergence

00:00:51.650 --> 00:00:58.525
Alternating positive and negative one stays bounded, yet its two subsequences settle at different values

00:00:58.525 --> 00:01:05.400
No single small neighborhood can contain the entire tail, so this sequence does not converge.
